From 2c2bae03845d1ebd9920d23ccf20b15f90258dc9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 15:10:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] dvbridge: do not blame the game mode alone for
 Low-Latency-only Dolby Vision
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The notification shown when a TV input offers Dolby Vision only in its
low-latency (player-led) form said that the input was in its game mode and
told the user to turn the game settings off for native Dolby Vision. That
holds for an LG C5 with Quick Frame Transport on, but not for a TV that has
no other form of Dolby Vision at all: the diagnostic of a 2020 Sony TV shows
a version 2 block of 12 bytes with interface 0 (low-latency YCbCr 4:2:2
only), and no setting of that TV is known to change it. There the text sent
the user looking for a setting that does not exist.

The text now says what happens (Dolby Vision plays as on a TV without it),
gives the game settings as a possible cause with the LG setting as the
example, and says that some TVs offer no other form. The English text is the
same in the core file (#39999) and in Estuary (#31689); es_es and es_ar keep
usted, es_mx keeps tú. The log line and the comments say the same.

When the notification is shown does not change: once for each state of the
display, as before.
